%% Every allowed transition is drawn and labelled with the command that causes
%% it. Anything not drawn is forbidden.
%%
%% One lifecycle, held by the room (ADR 0029): a Unit with no parent. A bed
%% under a room has no status of its own; its room answers for it. Whether a
%% Unit is blocked or out of service is accommodation_units.status, a
%% different lifecycle that coexists with this one — a blocked room is still
%% dirty, and still dirty after it is unblocked.
%%
%% A room somebody is staying in is cleaned on a schedule (slice 4, ADR 0063).
%% That is a second lifecycle and deliberately not a value of this one: "due" is
%% read from the schedule, the arrival and the last clean, so an occupied room
%% due for a clean is never dirty, never unready and never blocks a check-in.
%% It is drawn second, below.
%%
%% "Ready" is not a state. It is read off this one and the inspection setting:
%% inspected is ready; clean is ready when inspection is off. Changing the
%% setting therefore moves no room between states, and cannot strand one.
stateDiagram-v2

  state "Room — housekeeping status" as H {
    [*] --> Clean : add a Unit (no row yet, read as clean)
    Clean --> Dirty : check out
    Inspected --> Dirty : check out
    Dirty --> Dirty : check out (a second departure)
    Dirty --> Clean : mark clean
    Dirty --> Inspected : mark inspected
    Clean --> Inspected : mark inspected
    Clean --> Dirty : mark dirty
    Inspected --> Clean : mark clean
    Inspected --> Dirty : mark dirty
    %% Every mark is allowed from every state because a correction is one tap
    %% with no reason (HK-S2-05). Marking a room it is already in is allowed
    %% and audited, so a double submit is harmless (HK-S2-06).
    %% A check out makes the room dirty at once: a room a Guest has left since
    %% its status last changed reads dirty (app.unit_housekeeping_state), and
    %% the worker writes the row to match when it delivers the departure
    %% (HK-S1-22). The worker writes nothing when a person changed the status
    %% after the departure happened (HK-S1-05): the later word wins.
    %% No terminal state. A room is never deleted, and no status ends it.
  }

%% Stayover cleaning of a room somebody is in. Not stored: each state is read.
%% A clean is a row, undone by stamping it and never by deleting it, and only
%% on the day it was pressed (HK-S4-13, HK-S4-14).
  state "Room in use — stayover cleaning" as S {
    [*] --> NotDue : somebody arrives (the cycle begins)
    NotDue --> Due : the schedule's day comes round (every day, every N days, a weekday)
    Due --> Due : the day passes uncleaned (overdue, never lost)
    Due --> CleanedToday : mark cleaned
    CleanedToday --> Due : undo (same day only)
    CleanedToday --> NotDue : the day ends (the cycle begins again)
    NotDue --> NotDue : marked clean or inspected as turnover (the cycle begins again)
    Due --> NotDue : marked clean or inspected as turnover
    NotDue --> [*] : everybody leaves (turnover takes over)
    Due --> [*] : everybody leaves (turnover takes over)
  }
